<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Legio III Gallica</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 49 BCE–4th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Legio III Gallica was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legion</a> probably raised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> around 49 BCE. It served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>'s Parthian campaign and was long stationed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>; moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> in 68 CE, it destroyed a raiding force of some 9,000 Roxolani horsemen there early in 69 before declaring for Vespasian in the civil war. It returned to the East and is still recorded in the fourth century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Legio III Gallica, the Third Gallic Legion, was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> to help in his war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. Its soldiers were drawn exclusively from Transalpine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a>, and its cognomen Gallica suggests that its earliest recruits were veterans of Caesar's Gallic legions. Its emblem was a bull, a symbol associated with Caesar. After Caesar's death the legion joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>'s army and fought at Munda. It was part of the force levied by Fulvia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-antonius">Lucius Antonius</a> against Octavian, but surrendered at Perugia in the winter of 41 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The legion served in Antony's Parthian war in 36 BCE. After Actium and Antony's suicide it was sent east again and garrisoned the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. When Gnaeus Domitius Corbulo campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, between 58 and 68 CE, he drew the legion out with Legio VI Ferrata to form the core of his army. In 68 CE it was transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><p>In the Year of the Four Emperors, 69 CE, the Danubian army, the legion included, aligned first with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a> and then with Vespasian. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-vii-gemina">Legio VII Gemina</a> under Marcus Antonius Primus it marched on the supporters of Vitellius in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and fought at the Second Battle of Bedriacum. Having adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> the custom of saluting the rising sun, the legion turned east at dawn, and the Vitellian troops are said to have taken this for the arrival of reinforcements and lost heart. After advancing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the legion was sent back to Syria by Mucianus, who dispersed the units loyal to Primus.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> the legion was based at Raphanaea. Detachments may have fought in the Dacian wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>, and the legion probably took part in Trajan's Parthian wars. In 132 it was called on to crush the Jewish rebellion, and it later served in the Parthian campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-verus">Lucius Verus</a> and in that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 218 Julia Maesa went to Raphanaea, where the legion was commanded by Publius Valerius Comazon. She gave it large donations. At dawn on 16 May the legion proclaimed her fourteen-year-old grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabalus">Elagabalus</a> emperor. With the other eastern legions it helped defeat Macrinus near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> on 8 June. In 219, exhausted by Elagabalus's excesses, it supported its commander Verus, who proclaimed himself emperor; Elagabalus had Verus executed and dispersed the legion. Alexander Severus later reconstituted it and returned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and it took part in his campaign against the Sassanids. Its later history is obscure, and it was still active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the early fourth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Legio III Gallica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 69 CE some 9,000 Roxolani raiders were caught by the <b>Legio III Gallica</b> in Moesia on ground softened by a thaw, and Tacitus describes armoured riders unable to rise once unhorsed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Military</a></li></ul></section>
